```
Detection: Kerberoasting (RC4 service ticket requests)
MITRE ATT&CK: T1558.003 - Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Kerberoasting
Data source: Windows Security Event Log (index=winlogs)

Logic: TGS requests (4769) using RC4-HMAC (encryption type 23 / 0x17) are
abnormal in a modern domain where AES is the default, and are the classic
Kerberoasting signature: tickets encrypted with the service account's
NT hash can be cracked offline.

The encryption type is logged as hex text and the format varies between
sources ("0x17" vs "0x00000017"), so it is converted to a number instead of
string-matched. Computer accounts (ending in $) are excluded; roasting them
is pointless because their passwords are long and random.
```

index=winlogs EventCode=4769 Service_Name!="*$" Service_Name!="krbtgt"
| eval enc_type=tonumber(replace(Ticket_Encryption_Type, "^0x", ""), 16)
| where enc_type=23
| stats count dc(Service_Name) as distinct_services values(Service_Name) as services by Account_Name, src_ip
| where distinct_services >= 2 OR count > 3

```
False positives:
- Legacy applications/service accounts still configured for RC4 (a hygiene
  finding worth a ticket, not an active attack)

Analyst triage:
1. One account requesting RC4 tickets for many different services in a short
   window: high-confidence Kerberoasting, escalate.
2. A single known legacy service: raise a ticket to move it to AES.
3. Remediation: enforce AES-only Kerberos via GPO ("Network security:
   Configure encryption types allowed for Kerberos") and use long, random
   passwords (or gMSAs) for service accounts.
```
